## F2 — a second value token inside the single --tools group survives (FAIL)
checkPolicy line 226 reads `valuesAfter(argv, "--tools")[0]`, which is only the FIRST value of the
group. `--tools <tools...>` is variadic (claude --help). So a profile that emits
`"--tools", TOOLS, "WebFetch"` still has exactly one --tools flag, and the exactly-once loop passes.
The [0] read sees "Read,Grep,Glob,Bash" and the suite goes green.
  M10 "--tools", TOOLS, "WebFetch"  (start + resume)  exit=0  b1 0  b2 0  b3 0  b4 0
  M15 same shape, resume() only                      exit=0  b1 0  b2 0  b3 0  b4 0
This is a real widening on the installed vendor CLI. I captured the system/init event and killed
the process on init, so no model turn ran (script: /tmp/claude-1000/w044r2-ev/init-probe.mjs):
  baseline                              -> permissionMode=dontAsk tools=[Bash,Glob,Grep,Read]
  --tools Read,Grep,Glob,Bash WebFetch  -> permissionMode=dontAsk tools=[Bash,Glob,Grep,Read,WebFetch]
That is the same resolved tool set as M7, which round 1 failed. The suite's assertion "--tools is
exactly {Read,Grep,Glob,Bash}" is false for M10, and the assertion passes.
Why it blocks: the same ground as F1. The brief names "a widening flag" as the regression to keep
out, and claude-code.ts's header drops WebFetch as the profile's only egress.
Why round 1 missed it: my round-1 census counted reads of a flag's first OCCURRENCE. It did not
count reads of a group's first VALUE. My round-1 prescription ("each policy flag must occur
exactly once") would not kill it, and the builder implemented that prescription faithfully.
The literal-argv alternative I offered in round 1 would have killed it.

Class: "argv tokens the suite never accounts for". I checked every read site listed above, and
put a mutation after every flag position of the profile:
  1. line 226 --tools [0]: second token in the group. M10 and M15 survive. VENDOR-VERIFIED
     WIDENING. Blocking.
  2. line 225 --permission-mode [0]: M11 "dontAsk","acceptEdits" survives. The vendor init keeps
     permissionMode=dontAsk, so the extra token is a positional, not a widening. Advisory.
  3. Stray tokens after the boolean or single-value flags survive:
     M12 after --strict-mcp-config, M13 after --restricted, M14 after --output-format json.
     Init for M12/M13 is unchanged (dontAsk, [Bash,Glob,Grep,Read]). Not a widening. Advisory.
     The header's "message goes over stdin, never positional" contract is still unasserted.
  (--allowedTools is covered: its whole group is read and compared by set equality. Alias and
   equals-form flags are covered by b1/b4 set equality. Behaviours 2/3 read only the camelCase
   spelling, but b1 backstops that.)
One fix covers all three: assert that each form's argv deep-equals the literal expected array.
The only variables are the --append-system-prompt-file path (start) and the --resume session id
(resume). The file already writes the policy out literally, so that is a few lines. It kills
M5–M17 in one stroke. The minimum for the blocking instance alone would be reading --tools with
valuesAfterAll and requiring exactly one token. That leaves #2 and #3 open, so I recommend the
literal equality. claude-code.ts is correct today, and the fix stays inside the owned test file.

